\title{Fiat–Shamir and the Random Oracle Model}
\author{dhsorens}
\date{2026-08-18}

\p{
    The step that makes a proof system deployable, and the step where the security argument is thinnest.
}

\p{
    Questions this page should answer:
    \ul{
        \li{The transform, and exactly what it assumes about [the hash function](dhsorens-004D).}
        \li{Why [round-by-round soundness](dhsorens-002F) is the right hypothesis for a many-round protocol.}
        \li{Khovratovich, Rothblum, and Soukhanov's attack: GKR made non-interactive proves false statements. What it does and does not say about deployed provers.}
        \li{Duplex sponges versus hashing a transcript; domain separation and salting, and what ArkLib's \code{Salt.lean} is for.}
        \li{Whether the ROM is the right idealisation, and what the alternatives cost.}
    }
}

\p{
    Sources: [How to Prove False Statements](https://eprint.iacr.org/2025/118), [ArkLib](https://github.com/Verified-zkEVM/ArkLib) (\code{OracleReduction/FiatShamir}).
